+### 2207 — libssh2 live evidence closes Ed25519 and automatic-rekey review gaps (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
+- CONFORMANCE: A live OpenSSH fixture now authenticates an in-memory Ed25519 credential through the adapter's async `SSHPublicKeyCredential.sign` callback, records an actual opaque-signer invocation, preserves host-policy-before-credential ordering, carries only a Keychain-shaped credential reference, runs exec, and returns every owned resource to zero. The existing P-256 deployed-fallback coverage remains intact.
+- CONFORMANCE: Deterministic byte-threshold and injected-clock time-threshold tests drive the production libssh2 KEX path with an open channel, assert typed trigger/success events and `clientByteRekeys`/`clientTimeRekeys`, prove post-KEX traffic, and restore sessions, channels, sockets, tasks, allocations, and buffers to baseline after cancel/close.
+- FINDING: A full-duplex echo can legitimately cross the per-direction byte threshold twice—once for sent protected bytes and again for received protected bytes. The byte-threshold regression therefore uses bounded one-directional exec/stdin traffic to prove one exact trigger without weakening the per-direction policy or hiding a second valid KEX.
